From 6a7fa2cbefbbd6cb10ef1f1363a83d34bdd1c87b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 08:00:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 359/369] warden: stop wiping the persisted block count on
 every boot/restart

The total-blocked baseline lives on /jffs (RW_BASE, v2.2.1) so it should survive
reboot + reflash, but stop_rwarden() unconditionally unlinked it - and
stop_rwarden() is called by start_rwarden() (the stop-then-start idiom) on every
boot/restart and by stop_services() on shutdown. So the /jffs baseline was
deleted before Warden re-armed, and the count reset to zero on every reboot and
firmware upgrade (the v2.2.1 persistence fix never actually held; it was never
metal-validated).

Gate the reset on the live enable flag: preserve RW_BASE while Warden is enabled
(boot/restart/shutdown), unlink only on a genuine user-disable (the GUI sets
rwarden_enable=0 before it restarts us). A factory reset wipes /jffs -> zeroes it.

Rides v2.2.9 (unbuilt). Metal check: note the total, reboot, confirm it carries.
---
 release/src/router/rc/rwarden.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/rwarden.c b/release/src/router/rc/rwarden.c
index 54d4549047..8cb0d8b895 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/rwarden.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/rwarden.c
@@ -588,7 +588,16 @@ void stop_rwarden(void)
 	unlink(RW_APPLY);
 	unlink(RW_UPDATE);
 	unlink(RW_STATS);
-	unlink(RW_BASE);	/* disabling Warden resets the cumulative block count */
+	/* reaper v2.2.9 field fix (block count did not survive reboot/reflash): reset the
+	   cumulative baseline ONLY on a genuine user-DISABLE. stop_rwarden() is also called
+	   by start_rwarden() (the stop-then-start idiom) on every boot/restart AND by
+	   stop_services() on shutdown, so unconditionally unlinking RW_BASE here wiped the
+	   persisted /jffs baseline before Warden re-armed - silently defeating the v2.2.1
+	   "survives reboot+reflash" fix. Keyed on the live enable flag: enabled -> preserve
+	   (the GUI sets rwarden_enable=0 before it restarts us on a disable, so a real
+	   disable still resets; a factory reset wipes /jffs which zeroes it anyway). */
+	if (!nvram_match("rwarden_enable", "1"))
+		unlink(RW_BASE);
 }
 
 void start_rwarden(void)
